  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nRegional radar as of 29.1930Z shows the majority of warm \nadvective rain showers have moved into areas south of U.S. Hwy \n18 across southeast SD and northwest IA. These showers will \ncontinue to move eastward into the late afternoon/early evening \nwith most areas drying out by 00Z. That said, some CAMs show \nscattered convective activity re-developing along a trailing \nsurface boundary/front late this afternoon and evening mainly \nalong and south of a Yankton to Sioux Falls to Windom line. Area\nsoundings show some weak instability, so will keep chance \nPOPs/isolated thunder into the evening across far southeast SD \ninto northwest IA. Any lingering activity will diminish \novernight, although clouds will be slow to move out. Some \nclearing across southeast SD will allow lows to drop into the \nlow to mid 50s, whereas temperatures will bottom out closer to \n60 degrees for areas under cloud cover across northwest IA. \n\nIn addition, will need to watch for fog overnight. Given weak \nnear surface flow and ample boundary layer moisture from rain \ntoday, could see rapid fog development if the broader cloud \nshield dissipates or moves far enough east. \n\nFor Wednesday, a strong Four Corners closed low will begin to eject \neast-northeastward, and with it will be remnant moisture from \nHurricane Polo. This is a very wet system for late September with \nNAEFS PWATS above the 99th percentile. However, our southernmost \nforecast area will be on the northern periphery of the highest \nmoisture as the surface low moves across the Central Plains and the \nstationary front sets up across NE/IA. 00/06Z model runs last night \nand now the 12Z suite this morning have trended south with the \nhighest QPF. There is likely to be a sharp gradient in rainfall, \nranging from amounts less than an inch to higher than 2-3 inches. \nAreas north of U.S. Hwy 18 will likely remain dry all day. Current \ntrends suggest our forecast area will be on the lower end, but \ngiven some lingering concern about a late model shift north, \nwill keep current Flood Watch in place and adjust as needed on \nlater shifts. Otherwise, Wednesday will be warm and mostly \ncloudy with high temperatures in the low to mid 70s. \n\nSome lingering showers are possible Thursday across northwest IA, \nbut the majority of the area will be dry with much needed dry \nconditions continuing into Friday as surface high pressure moves \nacross the region. A cold frontal passage Wednesday night will also \nusher in cooler air, keeping high temperatures Thurs/Fri mainly in \nthe 60s. As long as skies remain mostly clear, overnight lows will \ndip into the mid to upper 40s. \n\nAnother weak wave moves through northwest flow aloft on Saturday, \nbringing a 20-40 percent chance of showers to areas mainly north of \nI-90. Synoptic 500 hPa flow begins to diverge between the major \nmodels Sunday into early next week, but the consensus now is for dry \nconditions with seasonable early October temperatures.",
